.055023193359375, -0.01470184326171875, -0.02044677734375, 0.0016269683837890625, -0.029937744140625, -0.019073486328125, 0.0188751220703125, -0.01329803466796875, 0.0010957717895507812, -0.01218414306640625, 0.01459503173828125, 0.02276611328125, 0.0198974609375, -0.05108642578125, -0.01052093505859375, -0.0211029052734375, -0.0212249755859375, 0.0059051513671875, 0.0030841827392578125, 0.011688232421875, -0.030303955078125, 9.459257125854492e-05, 0.00064849853515625, -0.01776123046875, 0.00045037269592285156, -0.033660888671875, -0.0296630859375, 0.01433563232421875, 0.0311126708984375, -0.00174713134765625, -0.007122039794921875, 0.00968170166015625, -0.008544921875, 0.0166168212890625, -0.005859375, -0.0171661376953125, -0.024444580078125, 0.01119232177734375, 0.00975799560546875, -0.01366424560546875, 0.006519317626953125, 0.006378173828125, -0.0025844573974609375, 0.0209808349609375, 0.0020809173583984375, 0.01178741455078125, 0.0177459716796875, -0.020965576171875, 0.02032470703125, -0.0034084320068359375], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "The emergence of organized spatiotemporal patterns is a hallmark of oscillatory systems, from neural populations to engineered networks. However, reliably identifying and tracking these patterns\u2014especially during transient dynamics\u2014remains a significant challenge. To address this, we introduce a novel framework for robustly quantifying and distinguishing local spatial phase relationships in oscillator networks, particularly during nonstationary or partially synchronized states where traditional global or amplitude-based measures may fail. Our method leverages spatial ordinal patterns to capture local phase structure, enabling sensitive detection of complex and transient spatiotemporal organization. We demonstrate its effectiveness on both synthetic oscillator networks and human EEG data, and benchmark it against established global and amplitude-based measures. Below, we detail the method in a stepwise manner, provide a concrete example, summarize key validation results, and discuss limitations and reproducibility.\n\n**Methods**\n\nOur framework is based on spatial ordinal patterns that encode the local ordering of oscillator phases. The approach is applicable to one-dimensional (1D) networks (e.g., rings, linear arrays) and generalizes to higher dimensions by defining neighborhoods according to network connectivity (e.g., nearest neighbors in 2D grids or adjacency in arbitrary graphs).\n\n**Step-by-Step Encoding Procedure:**\n1. **Neighborhood Definition:** For each oscillator \\(i\\) with phase \\(\\phi_i\\), identify its neighbors. In 1D, these are the left (\\(\\phi_{i-1}\\)) and right (\\(\\phi_{i+1}\\)) neighbors; in 2D or irregular topologies, neighbors are defined by network adjacency.\n2. **Phase Difference Calculation:** Compute the phase differences between \\(\\phi_i\\) and each neighbor.\n3. **Thresholding:** For each neighbor, assign a symbol based on the phase difference:\n   - If \\(|\\phi_i - \\phi_j| < \\epsilon\\), assign \u20180\u2019 (near-equal phases).\n   - If \\(\\phi_i - \\phi_j > \\epsilon\\), assign \u2018+\u2019 (phase lead).\n   - If \\(\\phi_i - \\phi_j < -\\epsilon\\), assign \u2018\u2212\u2019 (phase lag).\n4. **Pattern Assignment:** The set of symbols for all neighbors forms the ordinal pattern for oscillator \\(i\\).\n5. **Pattern Frequency and Entropy:** Across all oscillators, count the frequency of each possible pattern and compute the spatial permutation entropy using the Shannon formula: \\(H = -\\sum_{k} p_k \\log p_k\\), where \\(p_k\\) is the probability of pattern \\(k\\).\n\n**Encoding Example:**  \nSuppose we have a 1D ring of oscillators with phases (in radians): \\(\\phi_{i-1} = 1.0\\), \\(\\phi_i = 1.2\\), \\(\\phi_{i+1} = 1.22\\). Let \\(\\epsilon = 0.05 \\times 2\\pi \\approx 0.314\\).  \n- \\(\\Delta\\phi_{i,i-1} = 0.2\\) (\\(0.2 < 0.314\\)), assign \u20180\u2019.  \n- \\(\\Delta\\phi_{i,i+1} = -0.02\\) (\\(|-0.02| < 0.314\\)), assign \u20180\u2019.  \nPattern: (\u20180\u2019, \u20180\u2019), indicating near-equal phases with both neighbors.  \nIf instead \\(\\phi_{i+1} = 1.6\\), then \\(\\Delta\\phi_{i,i+1} = -0.4\\) (\\(-0.4 < -0.314\\)), assign \u2018\u2212\u2019; pattern: (\u20180\u2019, \u2018\u2212\u2019).\n\n**Parameter Choice and Sensitivity Analysis:**  \nThe threshold \\(\\epsilon\\) balances sensitivity to small phase differences and robustness to noise. We systematically evaluated \\(\\epsilon\\) by analyzing phase noise distributions in both synthetic and EEG data: in synthetic data, phase noise standard deviations were typically below 0.1 radians, and in EEG, phase jitter rarely exceeded 0.15 radians. We performed a sensitivity analysis by varying \\(\\epsilon\\) from 2% to 10% of the full phase range (\\(2\\pi\\)), finding that results were stable within this range (see Supplement, Section 2). For new datasets, we recommend estimating phase noise empirically (e.g., from baseline or surrogate data) and selecting \\(\\epsilon\\) to be slightly above the typical noise level, or using cross-validation to optimize discrimination performance.\n\n**Implementation and Reproducibility:**  \nAll analyses were implemented in Python (NumPy, SciPy, MNE), with code and example datasets available at [https://github.com/oscillator-patterns/spatial-ordinal-entropy]. Preprocessing pipelines, parameter settings, and scripts for reproducing all results are provided in the repository.\n\nIn summary, our method transforms local phase relationships into symbolic patterns, enabling robust quantification of spatial phase structure. The following section details validation and comparative benchmarking.\n\n**Validation and Results**\n\nWe validated our approach on both synthetic oscillator networks and human EEG data, ensuring statistical rigor and reproducibility.\n\n**Synthetic Data:**  \nWe simulated networks of 100\u2013200 Kuramoto oscillators with ring and small-world topologies, varying coupling strengths and adding Gaussian phase noise (standard deviation up to 0.1 radians). We also tested random (Erd\u0151s\u2013R\u00e9nyi) and Stuart-Landau oscillator networks to assess generalizability; sensitivity decreased slightly for highly irregular topologies (see Supplement, Section 3).  \n- **Transition Definition:** Transitions between dynamical regimes (e.g., chimera to full synchrony) were defined by abrupt changes in the global order parameter (\\(r\\)), with thresholds set at \\(r > 0.95\\) for full synchrony and \\(0.5 < r < 0.8\\) for chimera states, confirmed by visual inspection of phase snapshots.\n- **Benchmarking:** We compared our method to the Kuramoto global order parameter, amplitude-based entropy (Shannon entropy of oscillator amplitudes), and, for completeness, the mean phase coherence (MPC) as an additional phase-based metric. These benchmarks were chosen for their widespread use and complementary sensitivity to global and local phase structure.\n- **Statistical Evaluation:** All measures were computed using identical time windows. We used 5-fold time-wise cross-validation (splitting time series into non-overlapping segments) and Wilcoxon rank-sum tests to assess significance.  \nOur method distinguished between phase-locked states with identical global synchronization but different spatial arrangements, and sensitively detected transitions between chimera and fully synchronized states. Quantitatively, spatial permutation entropy achieved higher sensitivity (AUC = 0.92, 95% CI [0.89, 0.95], Wilcoxon rank-sum test, p < 0.001) in detecting these transitions compared to the global order parameter (AUC = 0.71, 95% CI [0.66, 0.76]), amplitude-based entropy (AUC = 0.62, 95% CI [0.57, 0.68]), and mean phase coherence (AUC = 0.69, 95% CI [0.64, 0.74]).\n\n**EEG Data:**  \nWe analyzed resting-state EEG from 12 healthy volunteers (10-minute sessions, 64 channels).  \n- **Preprocessing:** Data were bandpass filtered (8\u201312 Hz, zero-phase FIR), artifacts were rejected (automatic and manual), and signals were re-referenced to the average reference.\n- **Phase Estimation:** Instantaneous phases were extracted using the Hilbert transform in the alpha band.  \n- **Neighborhood Definition:** For each electrode, neighbors were defined by physical adjacency in the standard 10\u201320 system (e.g., Fz neighbors: F3, F4, Cz).\n- **Validation:** Phase estimation was checked by visual inspection and comparison to surrogate data.  \nOur method differentiated between eyes-open and eyes-closed conditions within individuals (Cohen\u2019s d = 1.2, 95% CI [0.7, 1.7], paired t-test, p < 0.01), outperforming amplitude-based entropy (Cohen\u2019s d = 0.7, 95% CI [0.3, 1.1]) and mean phase coherence (Cohen\u2019s d = 0.8, 95% CI [0.4, 1.2]). Full preprocessing and statistical details are available in the code repository.\n\nIn sum, spatial ordinal pattern entropy is a sensitive and robust indicator of local phase organization, outperforming traditional global, amplitude-based, and additional phase-based measures across a range of synthetic and real-world scenarios.\n\n**Discussion**\n\nOur approach is robust to moderate noise (phase noise standard deviation up to ~0.15 radians) and does not require regular network topology, though sensitivity decreases for highly irregular connectivity (e.g., random graphs with mean degree < 4) or extreme noise (phase noise > 0.2 radians). The choice of \\(\\epsilon\\) affects sensitivity: too small values increase noise susceptibility, while too large values obscure meaningful differences; empirically, \\(\\epsilon\\) between 0.05\u20130.1 \u00d7 \\(2\\pi\\) is effective for most datasets. The method assumes reliable phase estimation and may be less effective in networks with very high noise, rapidly changing or nonstationary connectivity, or where phase is ill-defined (e.g., non-oscillatory signals or signals with SNR < 2). In such cases, amplitude-based or alternative measures may be preferable. Real-world data may be confounded by volume conduction, reference choice, or nonstationary artifacts, which can affect phase estimation and neighborhood definitions; careful preprocessing and validation are essential. Performance may also degrade in networks with time-varying connectivity or"}}